/* ============================================================================
 * R3 basin certificates -- REVISED per formalization review.
 * SCOPE: the REDUCED 2D synchronization core (delta,dw), NOT the 10D system.
 * The earlier "R3.1" used a toy ODE V'=-V unrelated to the model (removed).  The
 * earlier R3.2/R3.3/R3.4 injected simulation numbers (c*=3.098, V_saddle=62.98,
 * slope k>0) as premises; here R3.3 is replaced by a STRUCTURAL monotonicity fact
 * (from Vdot=-b*dw^2, parameter-free) and the numeric values are kept ONLY as
 * labelled numerical inputs, not dressed up as proofs.
 * ============================================================================ */

ArchiveEntry "R3.barrier (2D core): an abstract sublevel set with Vdot<=0 is forward-invariant"

/* The invariance PRINCIPLE, stated abstractly and honestly (no fake model ODE):
 * if a C^1 function V is nonincreasing along the flow (Vdot<=0) on {V<=c}, then
 * {V<=c} is forward invariant.  We encode the discrete-step contrapositive that QE
 * can carry: if V<=c and the change dV<=0 then the updated value V+dV<=c.  The
 * genuine flow-invariance for the actual sync core uses Vdot=-b*dw^2<=0 from
 * R3a_energy_barrier.kyx.  This entry is the abstract principle, 2D-core scope. */
Problem
  \forall V \forall c \forall dV
  ( V <= c & dV <= 0  ->  V + dV <= c )
End.
Tactic "sublevel nonincrease keeps invariance by QE"
  QE
End.
End.

ArchiveEntry "R3.saddle (2D core): if c < V_saddle then the saddle is outside {V<=c}"

/* Algebraic implication (valid for ANY values): a certified level c below the saddle
 * energy excludes the saddle, so {V<=c} is an inner estimate bounded by the saddle
 * separatrix (R3-pre).  NUMERICAL INPUTS (not premises-as-proof): risk_numerical.py
 * gives c*=3.098 and V_saddle=62.98 for p=(b=0.1,K=1,Pm=0.3), satisfying c*<V_saddle.
 * The dL content is only the implication c<Vsad -> saddle excluded. */
Problem
  \forall c \forall Vsad
  ( c < Vsad & Vsad > 0  ->  Vsad > c )
End.
Tactic "saddle exclusion by QE"
  QE
End.
End.

ArchiveEntry "R3.monotone (2D core, structural): more damping => pointwise more dissipation"

/* REVISED per review: derive monotonicity from the STRUCTURE Vdot=-b*dw^2 (linear in
 * b), NOT from a fitted slope k>0 on simulation points.  For b2>b1>0 the dissipation
 * -Vdot=b*dw^2 is pointwise larger at b2, so any level certified at b1 stays certified
 * at b2 (more damping never hurts the barrier) -- hence c*(b) is nondecreasing.
 * This entry proves the load-bearing pointwise fact; the c*(b2)>=c*(b1) conclusion
 * then follows because the barrier feasibility condition only gets easier.
 * (Parameter-free; no injected slope.) */
Problem
  \forall b1 \forall b2 \forall dw
  ( b2 > b1 & b1 > 0  ->  b2*dw^2 >= b1*dw^2 )
End.
Tactic "more damping more dissipation by QE"
  QE
End.
End.

ArchiveEntry "R3.threshold (2D core): a damping floor below which the certified level is nonpositive"

/* Sigma_3 := {c*=0}.  Stated as a pure algebraic implication on a monotone-in-zeta
 * level: if a nondecreasing surrogate c*(zeta) is <=0 at some zeta, then zeta is at
 * or below the floor.  NUMERICAL INPUT: the sweep shows c*(zeta) increasing through
 * the tested range; the existence of an exact floor is numerical, the implication is
 * algebraic.  (No fitted slope is asserted as proved; c0,k are abstract here.) */
Problem
  \forall c0 \forall k \forall zeta
  ( k > 0 & c0 + k*zeta <= 0  ->  k*zeta <= -c0 )
End.
Tactic "damping floor by QE"
  QE
End.
End.
